:"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer gets the main comparison right. It says a series trust reduces startup burdens (trust formation, board governance, compliance, vendor coordination) and provides established servicing. It also says this can outweigh fees for one conventional ETF on a limited budget, while a standalone trust gives more control but carries fixed costs that suit sponsors expecting scale. It avoids a universal winner, consistent with key point 3 and the grader notes. Two substantive omissions prevent a correct grade. First, the control trade-off is only described generically (control over branding, policies, vendors, timing). It never says the platform's board and officers are not the sponsor's, nor that this board can replace the adviser or liquidate the fund, which is the core of the control risk. Second, it does not mention that the sponsor would typically act as adviser, sub-adviser or contractual partner. The point that fixed governance and servicing costs are spread across many funds is implied but not stated. No material errors.\"}","ttft_ms":2890,"type":"result","duration_ms":5954,"uuid":"39af860e-11bc-4924-b3e9-8d3c77da687f","ttft_stream_ms":560,"time_to_request_ms":59,"first_content_frame_ms":560,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
